cast","title":"2020 Reunion","body":"Hum reunited in 2020 to release *Inlet*, their first album in 22 years. This marked a significant return for the band after years of independent work. \"And in 2020, they got back together again and released a record called Inlet. This album in 22 years.\"","year":2020,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Great Alt-Rock One-Hit Wonders of the 90s: Part 2","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SOAL0GODN3M"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Dropped by RCA","body":"After the success of \"Stars\" and *You'd Prefer an Astronaut*, subsequent singles from the album, specifically \"Stift,\" failed to perform well. Following a second record for RCA, the band was dropped by the label and reverted to working as an indie band. \"A couple of other singles from the album Stift, and after a second record for RCA, they were dropped and have been working as an indie band ever since.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Great Alt-Rock One-Hit Wonders of the 90s: Part 2","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SOAL0GODN3M"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Formation and Early Indie Work","body":"Hum has existed in various forms since 1989, releasing music on an indie label starting in 1991 before securing a major label deal with RCA in 1995. They are from Champagne, Illinois. \"They've been around in one form or another, not counting various breaks since 1989.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Great Alt-Rock One-Hit Wonders of the 90s: Part 2","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SOAL0GODN3M"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Influence on Other Bands","body":"Hum's album *You'd Prefer an Astronaut* and its lead single \"Stars\" have been cited as highly influential by other bands. For instance, the Deftones stated that the album was crucial to the development of their sound. Other bands like The Smashing Pumpkins and Sonic Youth are also mentioned as part of the \"heavy fuzzy bands\" influenced by Hum. \"The Deftones, for example, say that you'd prefer an astronaut was a big deal to them and how they developed their sound.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Great Alt-Rock One-Hit Wonders of the 90s: Part 2","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SOAL0GODN3M"}]}],"awards":[],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":242726677894795,"type":"album","name":"You'd Prefer an Astronaut","list_id":118267099057662,"list":"AOTY Must Hear 1990s","src":"musthear","rank":240,"year":1995,"note":null,"yt":"c4UUessm_9U","scaruffi":null},{"id":242726677894795,"type":"album","name":"You'd Prefer an Astronaut","list_id":84403038516466,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Alternative Rock","src":"musthear","rank":128,"year":1995,"note":null,"yt":"c4UUessm_9U","scaruffi":null},{"id":242726677894795,"type":"album","name":"You'd Prefer an Astronaut","list_id":79201857171461,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Hardcore [Punk]","src":"musthear","rank":34,"year":1995,"note":null,"yt":"c4UUessm_9U","scaruffi":null},{"id":242726677894795,"type":"album","name":"You'd Prefer an Astronaut","list_id":15546028624161,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Post-Hardcore","src":"musthear","rank":25,"year":1995,"note":null,"yt":"c4UUessm_9U","scaruffi":null},{"id":242726677894795,"type":"album","name":"You'd Prefer an Astronaut","list_id":78805621635440,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Punk","src":"musthear","rank":84,"year":1995,"note":null,"yt":"c4UUessm_9U","scaruffi":null},{"id":177877940924083,"type":"album","name":"downward is heavenward","list_id":118267099057662,"list":"AOTY Must Hear 1990s","src":"musthear","rank":91,"year":1998,"note":null,"yt":"HsNyU0kkJaM","scaruffi":null},{"id":177877940924083,"type":"album","name":"downward is heavenward","list_id":84403038516466,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Alternative Rock","src":"musthear","rank":156,"year":1998,"note":null,"yt":"HsNyU0kkJaM","scaru